Android host mode for Madeira's FEX fork (willfaust/FEX @ f59cab95).

The fork is built for iOS (FEX_IOS_HOST). Its iOS-only machinery (TEB through
TPIDRRO_EL0/TSD, the dual-mapped JIT pool and its PE-image aliases, the pool
sweeper, Mach exception quirks) cannot work on Android, where Wine runs as on
Linux: x18 is the TEB, anonymous memory can be RWX, exceptions arrive as
signals. FEX_ANDROID_HOST keeps upstream FEX's Linux design for those and
turns on the fork's host-independent parts that Madeira's Wine relies on:

- JIT guard page: a fault is only a guard-page hit once the thread has a
  guard page (a null-pointer fault before the first compile used to
  "restart" through an unset jump buffer and resume at pc 0).
- Dispatcher: after SleepThread, resume the guest instead of hlt(0), which
  under Wine's exception path falls into CallbackPtr (fork ml325).
- WoW64: honour WOW64_CPURESERVED_FLAG_RESET_STATE, which is how Madeira's
  wow64 asks for the 32-bit registers to be reloaded after SetThreadContext.
- Phone-sized per-thread lookup cache (2 MB L1 cap, 32 MB block arena),
  committed up front as on iOS rather than on its first access violation.
- ExitToX64: native short-circuit of ARM64EC fast-forward sequences (x18
  TEB, no pool aliases), so an EC function reached through its x64 entry
  stub never enters the emulator.
- CheckCall (the call checker FEX installs for its own ntdll calls): a target
  that is not in ntdll's redirection table is kept as is. Madeira's loader
  binds FEX's imports to the native implementations already, and the plain
  lookup turned those into ntdll's base address.

- Guards that hold on 16 KB page kernels, where Wine merges the 4 KB
  protections of each host page so a lone 4 KB no-access page stays
  accessible: call-ret stack guards of 64 KB (an imbalance in the WoW64
  module, which has no inline bounds check, ran past the allocation instead
  of being reset), and a 64 KB inaccessible tail behind the JIT buffer's
  guard page (an overflow faulted past the page the restart handler checks).

- No implicit thread-local storage in the Windows modules (IR capture mark,
  allocation watch), as the fork already does on iOS: in the WoW64 module the
  64-bit TEB has no ThreadLocalStoragePointer, so the first block compile of
  every 32-bit program read through a null pointer; in the ARM64EC module the
  variable landed in the game's own TLS block.

and supplies non-iOS fallbacks where the fork uses iOS helpers outside their
guards: identity PE-alias translation, no code-buffer sweep (and so no WoW64
syscall parking, which only exists for the sweeper), zero VA band, no
C-runtime time() in a JIT diagnostic, and no iOS-only exports in the .def.

Built with -DFEX_ANDROID_HOST by runtime/android/build-fex.sh (fex-madeira).
